it's been called the death of cambodia's  democracy a court in the capital phnom  penh has jailed dozens of opposition  figures they were convicted of  conspiring to commit treason rights  groups say the entire process was a sham  accusing long-time leader hun sen of  using the mass trial to crack down on  dissent and hold on to power  in a trial aimed at suppressing  dissenting voices terry sang one of the  accused made it clear she had come to  defend liberty  i am freedom  i am your breath  you cannot live well without me  across the road in a courtroom seng and  60 other dissidents many of them already  in exile were being tried for treason  the court alleges they helped plot the  return of the leader of the banned  cambodia national rescue party  once the ruling party's main rival  i am ready  for the sham verdict that will be  announced this morning which will be a  guilty verdict i am ready and prepared  to go to the notorious cambodian prisons  for my political opinions for my beliefs  i for my belief in democracy  as the verdict was announced seng's  prediction was borne out  she was found guilty and sentenced to  six years in prison  others received sentences of five to  eight years  shortly afterwards she was arrested and  bundled into a police car  scuffles then erupted between her  supporters and police  cambodian courts are widely believed to  be under the influence of prime minister  hun sen  seen here at local elections just over a  week ago  he has been leading cambodia for 37  years human rights groups say with  increasingly repressive methods  sen has effectively turned cambodia into  a one-party state by arresting and  prosecuting his critics  this trial appears to be his latest  attempt to stamp out any remaining  opposition  we can go now to bangkok and speak to  phil robertson the deputy director of  the asia division of human rights watch  phil welcome to dw news asia  you've spoken out very forcefully on the  conviction of these opposition party  members in cambodia on twitter today you  called it an outrageous violation of  rights could you run through exactly  what you mean  well these people have been hit with  trumped-up charges uh and basically  dragged in front of a court that is not  independent it is a kangaroo court that  does the bidding of uh the ruling party  and the prime minister of cambodia and  what we're seeing is um no real evidence  produced at these trials to prove the  cases of incitement uh or conspiracy  that these people are being accused of  uh and have now been convicted of  again it is just more of the same from  cambodia it's a single party state  that is using uh  unfair laws and and unjust courts to uh  go after the opposition and put them  away for long prison terms it is  again an action by the cambodian  government to try to wipe out what  remains of the political opposition and  further narrow uh political space in the  country  now the u.s cambodian lawyer and  campaigner terry singh who was one of  those convicted and we just saw her in  that piece she says people in cambodia  are living in a dictatorship now is she  right  oh she's absolutely right i mean it's  quite clear that the cambodian  government is becoming more dictatorial  by the day  rights abuses are expanding across the  country uh there is no political  opposition uh elected political  opposition really to speak of  uh you know hun sen and uh his his party  and his government uh basically control  all the levers of power and they're not  afraid to go after anyone that makes a  criticism on facebook or otherwise says  something negative about the government  now you just mentioned prime minister  hun sen what means do the international  community have to pressure him over this  and how likely are they to use them  well i think we need to see more  pressure on trade uh with cambodia uh  the european union has an existing uh  uh  everything but arms agreement with  cambodia and 20 of that was suspended  before the pandemic hit because of uh  labor rights violations i think that the  european union should go back to that  again and look at taking more uh frankly  uh cambodia is in clear violation of the  terms of the uh the eba agreement that  requires cambodia to uh follow existing  international human rights treaties and  labor rights conventions and and  cambodia is doing none of it  now critics of hong sen say that over  many years he has eliminated his critics  but specifically uh used the pandemic to  do this too  to what extent did he use covert 19 to  expand his authoritarian control  well he passed a an emergency law that  is breathtaking its scope it has not  been invoked but it  essentially allows him to do anything he  wants if he invokes that law  and he has used uh various different  provisions uh of law  against people who are criticizing him  on facebook saying that the covent  pandemic response in cambodia was not up  to snuff that for instance uh some of  the vaccines that they had received from  certain countries were not good  uh you know all the criticism that was  made online you know if you said  something on facebook about what the  prime minister is doing on covid you  could expect the next day or two that  the police wouldn't be knocking on your  door and that is the new reality in  cambodia that online surveillance  is now matched with real repression on  the ground with by the police and the  military who are completely in hoonsen's  camp  a pretty dim picture of this new reality  as you so describe it phil  more generally what's the state of play  when it comes to human rights in  cambodia  well we are seeing uh civil and  political rights being violated on a  regular basis this is freedom of  expression freedom of association it's  basically impossible to hold a uh  political protest in cambodia in the  capital city of ponom pen  uh people who are campaigning against uh  dams against land seizures against other  environmental issues that you know  affect people's basic lives are are  facing arrests  uh you know it's across the board uh  that we've seen a real tightening of  repression against anybody who uh raises  criticism against the government in  cambodia  that was phil robertson the deputy  director of the asia division of human  rights watch phil thank you very much
 

